<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivo of Kermartin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1253">1253</a>–<a href="/years/1303">1303</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Saint Yves, Saint Ivo of Kermartin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivo of Kermartin, known as Saint Yves, was a Breton priest and church judge of the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treguier">Tréguier</a>, famed for defending the poor without fee. He died in 1303 and was canonised in 1347. He is the patron saint of Brittany and of lawyers, and his feast on 19 May draws a great pardon procession at Tréguier.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivo-of-kermartin.jpg" alt="Ivo of Kermartin" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Rogier van der Weyden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weyden_Ivo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivo was born at Kermartin, a manor near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treguier">Tréguier</a> in Brittany, on 17 October 1253, the son of Helori, lord of Kermartin, and Azo du Kenquis. In 1267 he was sent to the Faculty of Law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a>, where he graduated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>. While other students caroused, he studied, prayed and visited the sick, and he refused to eat meat or drink wine. Among his fellow students were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duns-scotus">Duns Scotus</a> and Roger Bacon. In 1277 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> to study canon law under Peter de la Chapelle, who later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and a cardinal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in Brittany, having received minor orders, Ivo was appointed in 1280 official, the title of an ecclesiastical judge, of the archdeanery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a>. He protected orphans and widows, defended the poor and gave fair and impartial verdicts. He represented the helpless in other courts, paid their expenses and visited them in prison. Although it was usual to give judges gifts, he refused bribes, and he often helped disputing parties settle out of court to save them money. A tradition among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> holds that he joined the Third Order of St Francis at Guingamp.</p><p>Ivo was ordained a priest in 1284 and accepted an invitation from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treguier">Tréguier</a> to become his official. He did not hesitate to resist taxation by the king, which he considered an encroachment on the rights of the Church. In 1285 he was appointed to the parish of Tredrez, and eight years later he was sent to Louannec, where he built a hospital and helped to care for the sick. In a story retold by John Wigmore in 1936, he saved a widow at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> from ruin by pointing out that the merchant suing her could not claim a casket that two men were to demand together.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ivo died on 19 May 1303 of natural causes, after a life of hard work and repeated fasting, and was buried in Minihy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treguier">Tréguier</a> in the church he had founded. At the inquest into his sanctity in 1331 many parishioners testified that he preached regularly in chapel and field and that under him the people of the land became twice as good as before. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vi">Pope Clement VI</a> canonised him in June 1347, at the urging of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-duke-of-burgundy">Philip I, Duke of Burgundy</a>. He is patron of Brittany, lawyers and abandoned children, and law schools and Catholic lawyers' associations have taken his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivo of Kermartin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The church was organised in nine dioceses, and local saints’ cults and pilgrimages marked religious life; <b>Ivo of Kermartin</b>, a priest and judge who died in 1303, was canonised in 1347 as Saint Yves, later patron of lawyers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Louannec, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.79,-3.41&amp;year=1285" aria-label="Louannec on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Ivo of Kermartin"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4808 5947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4808 5947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48.79,-3.41&amp;year=1285">What was here in 1285? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
